+
+**Three kinds of record, one rule each.** (1) **Line records** (the status file, the run parameters) and **the briefs**: the header holds the values; the prose line stays, because the owner, the supervisor and every instruction read it (`Phase: changing`, the `## Scope` section the builder is told to stay inside); a program reads the header; the record's own checker compares the header with the prose and fails a disagreement (`HEADER DISAGREES: <key>`), so the two cannot drift silently. (2) **Structured records** (the plan): the same rule — the header holds every increment's fields, the increment's prose keeps its heading, its `Author:` and its `Done when:` and its field lines; `plan-check.py` compares the header's fields with the prose field lines where the prose has them, under the stated normalisations (`parallel`'s `yes` and `no` against `true` and `false`; a stage's `demonstration` against the presence of its `Demonstration:` line; an increment's `title` against its heading's title; `kind` against `increment` and `parallel` against `false` where the `Kind:` or `Parallel:` line is absent, the fallback's defaults, which a historic increment carries; an optional scalar's line absent against `null`, an optional list's line absent — `Scope:`, `Depends on:`, `Diagrams:`, `Satisfies:` — against the empty list, the fallback's reading today, so a historic increment's lists are empty and never null, and a `Parent:`, `Depends on:` or `Diagrams:` line holding the filler `none` as an absent line; `historic`, which has no line, by its derivation; a `scope` entry the bullet's path as `split_entry` reads it, a folder's trailing `/` kept on both sides and never added or removed), so an edit to one without the other fails at the next checkpoint. The plan's header is the one record whose size is worth a sentence: for this repository, fifty-four increments, by estimate somewhat over a tenth of the file; the plan is read whole at planning and by `spec-packager`, and the growth is measured and recorded at the migration (Testing strategy). (3) **Tabular records** (the inventory, the register, the index, the deviations): a header cannot hold rows without copying the table, so it declares the table's shape — the sections, the columns in order, the key or role columns — and the programs split each row by the declared columns, never by position guessed from a header row's first word; the checker compares the declared columns with the table's own header row and fails a difference (`HEADER DISAGREES: columns`, or `HEADER DISAGREES: roles.<role>` where a role's column is absent from the table's header row, a finding, exit 1; a `roles` value naming a column absent from the header's own `columns` is malformed, a value rule `check_header` holds); each table has one owning checker: the inventory `inventory-check.py` (`HEADER DISAGREES: tables.<section>`, `development_data`, `reflected_through` too), the register `completion-check.py`, the index and the deviations file `diagram-check.py`, the other reader of each reading the header and trusting the owner's check; the one value a tabular header copies from a row is the inventory's `development_data`. The comparison in every kind is made by the fallback reader: *agreement* means the fallback's reading of the prose equals the header, so the one piece of code that reads prose is the one that migrated it and the one that checks it.
+
+**The fallback readers, one copy each.** Today the prose readers are private copies in eight programs, and eleven fields have two readings. The release moves each reader into `bridge_env.py` as the one copy, unchanged in rule where there is one, and where there are two it keeps the one named here, the other program's copy retired (a test greps each program for its retired regex): `Phase:` — the lenient `Key: value` regex of `loop-guard.py`'s and `session-start.py`'s `field()` (a bullet, bold, underscores and backticks tolerated), while `workflow-check.py`'s exact `Phase: ` at the line's start stays what it is, a **placement** rule on the prose line, not a reading of the value, and `conformance-check.py`'s two readings of the value are retired (its `status_field` regex, which stops at a backtick, applied to `Phase:`, and its grades' `line.startswith("Phase: ")`; the grades read the one value, the header's or the fallback's); `Awaiting user on:` — `loop-guard.py`'s: a value that, stripped of `<` and `>`, starts with `nothing`, `none` or `n/a` in any case is no wait, normalised to `""`; `Bridge version:` — `session-start.py`'s: the first `YYYY.MM.DD` with an optional letter found in the value, else `""`; `Workflows:` — `workflow-check.py`'s strict grammar (`check_status`), a line it refuses a finding, never a silent none (`session-start.py`'s forgiving parse retired); `Open issues:` — `evaluate-reports.py`'s `status_segments`; `Stage:` — `conformance-check.py`'s `status_field`, the one reader; the plan's increment headings and fields — `plan-check.py`'s `HEAD_RX` and `FIELD_RX` inside an increment, over every `docs/BUILD_PLAN*.md` (so `bridge_env.plan_increment_ids` reads every plan file), a `Scope:` line split on `;` as today, with one refusal added: an entry with an unbalanced parenthesis (a parenthesis holding a semicolon, which the split halves) is reported by the reader, a shape failure in `plan-check.py` for an unmerged increment (a merged increment's Scope is history, read as today and never read for a brief again) and `cannot read <path>: unbalanced parenthesis in <id>'s Scope` in the migration for any increment, since a header would make the misreading permanent; and `Satisfies:` by that same regex inside the increment, `completion-check.py`'s whole-text search retired — with the agreement rule that every R-id the retired search would have found in a plan file stands in some increment's header `satisfies`, so a bold `**Satisfies**:` line outside an increment that counted yesterday is a `HEADER DISAGREES: satisfies` today and not a silent loss; the rule's sweep is one function in `bridge_env` (`plan_r_ids_outside_headers(text)`: every R-id in the plan file's body outside the header, by the one R-id pattern the register reader uses), which `completion-check.py` calls, so the program keeps no whole-text `Satisfies` regex of its own, the retired reading, and the currency test, which greps the programs and not `bridge_env`, has nothing to exempt; a stage's heading and its `Demonstration:` line — `plan-check.py`'s, compared with the header's `name` and `demonstration` like an increment's fields; a brief's `## Scope` — `scope-check.py`'s `parse_scope`, with its refusals (an ellipsis, an empty Scope, an unreadable file), `plan-check.py`'s `brief_scope` retired; a brief's `Satisfies:` line — the one new reading, the template's line read as `FIELD_RX` reads the plan's; `Merge unit:` and `Blocking class:` — `bridge_env`'s readers, as today; the inventory's tables — `inventory-check.py`'s exact section names and first-row header, `conformance-check.py`'s heading-word guess retired; the register — `completion-check.py`'s `parse_register`, the R-ids `diagram-check.py` traces read from the id column of the rows it gives, `diagram-check.py`'s whole-text `R_RX` over the register retired; the index and the deviations — `diagram-check.py`'s header-checked reads, `plan-check.py`'s column-position read of the index retired; `Ceiling above twice:` — `bridge-run.py`'s `owner_cap` regex, one line per kind with a non-empty parenthesis required, `loop-guard.py`'s copy retired; `Agent ceiling:` and `Design rounds above five:` — their one reader each, moved. The one copy is what `records-migrate.py` reads to write a header, what the checker reads to compare, and what a program reads for a record with no header. **The retired readings, enumerated** — the one list that Impact, R-057, TASK-047's grep and the currency test (TASK-050) cite, each a program's use of a pattern, matched as a use (a `re` call's argument or the name bound to it, docstrings and comments stripped with `ast`, so that a comment naming the design never matches, KP-073) and never quoted in a program's prose: (1) `session-start.py`'s forgiving `Workflows:` parse; (2) `session-start.py`'s equality of `Awaiting user on:` with its four exact values (`nothing`, `none`, the empty string, `-`); (3) `conformance-check.py`'s `Bridge version:` regex (`[0-9.]+[a-z]?`); (4) `conformance-check.py`'s `status_field` applied to `Phase:` and its grades' `line.startswith("Phase: ")`; (5) `completion-check.py`'s whole-text `Satisfies` search over the plan; (6) `plan-check.py`'s `brief_scope`; (7) `conformance-check.py`'s heading-word guess of the inventory's sections; (8) `plan-check.py`'s column-position read of the index; (9) `diagram-check.py`'s whole-text `R_RX` over the register; (10) `loop-guard.py`'s `Ceiling above twice:` regex; (11) `plan-check.py`'s own `merged_ids` (below, the one merged set).
+
+**Header first; fallback; malformed.** A record whose first line is `---` has a header, read to the next `---` line. A record whose first line is anything else has none, and the program reads it as today (the fallback, today's code kept whole). A header the reader cannot parse, or that fails its schema (an unknown key, a missing key, a wrong type, a value outside its enumeration, a `record:` kind the program did not expect, a block over its bound) is **malformed**, and every reader has a stated outcome: a **checker** (`plan-check.py`, `scope-check.py`, `inventory-check.py`, `completion-check.py`, `diagram-check.py`, `conformance-check.py`, `workflow-check.py`) prints `HEADER MALFORMED: <path>:<line> <what>` and exits 2, the cannot-verify exit `scope-check.py`, `conformance-check.py` and `workflow-check.py` have today and the four checkers that exit 0 or 1 today (`plan-check.py`, `completion-check.py`, `inventory-check.py`, `diagram-check.py`) gain for this case, never silently falling back, since a typo would otherwise switch a record's parsing without a word, while `HEADER DISAGREES: <key>` is a finding and exits as the checker's findings do (1), but in `scope-check.py`, where it exits 2 as an unreadable Scope does, since the checker then cannot tell which scope to verify; a **hook** (`loop-guard.py`, `session-start.py`) falls back to the prose, as it fails open on every other error today, `session-start.py` printing one plain line `header malformed: <path>:<line>` beside its pointers, and the next checkpoint's `inventory-check.py` fails the record (a **disagreement** the hooks see as well, since both readings are in hand: `session-start.py` prints `header disagrees: <key>` the same way for each status key it reads — `phase`, `awaiting_user_on`, `bridge_version`, `workflows` — where the header's value differs from the fallback's reading of the line, the header's value governing the modules it names, since a resumed session should learn of the drift before the checkpoint; `loop-guard.py` refuses the stop with `header disagrees: <key>` where the status file's `phase` or `awaiting_user_on`, or the run parameters' `agent_ceiling` or `ceiling_above_twice`, differs from its line, as it refuses a stop on other grounds, since an allowed stop on a drifted header is KP-031's class, and the supervisor fixes the record); the **launcher and the guard** (`bridge-run.py` and `review-guard.py`, each on a brief's header and on the run parameters', the guard reading the branch's first brief for its `merge_unit` as the launcher does, its `Blocking class:` line being the SCOPE file's, which carries no header) refuse with exit 125 and a message beginning `bridge-run: refused - header: ` or `review-guard: refused - header: `, a stop and not a failure (the loop's step 3b, as their other refusals), since a gate program must not guess which of two readings an owner's lift line has, and both refuse the same way a header that disagrees with its prose (`bridge-run: refused - header: disagrees <key>`, `review-guard: refused - header: disagrees <key>`), each comparing the keys it reads: a brief's, every key by the launcher before the launch, as it reads the brief for the round and the merge unit, so a builder is never launched on a brief whose `## Scope` and `scope` differ, and `merge_unit`, `id` and `round` by the guard before a snapshot, the keys it reads of the branch's first brief for the branch rule; and the run parameters', the lifts each reads (the launcher `ceiling_above_twice`, `design_rounds_above_five` and `review_rounds_above_ceiling`; the guard `review_rounds_above_ceiling`) compared with the lines before a lift governs a launch or a snapshot, so a lift the owner never wrote on a line never lifts a ceiling; the **evaluation** (`evaluate-reports.py`, a report run by the maintainer) falls back to the prose and writes `header malformed: <path>:<line>` into its report beside the project. The header is never read past the first `---`...`---` block, and the body is everything after it; a `---` line later in a file (a Markdown rule) is body.
+
+**The reader.** One reader in `bridge_env.py` (`read_header(text)` → the header as a mapping or `None`, and the body; `check_header(kind, header)` → the schema findings; `HEADER_SCHEMAS`), imported by every program above — `conformance-check.py` and `bridge-install.py` load `bridge_env` by file already and keep doing so. It reads a **stated subset of YAML**, enough for the schemas and no more: `key: value` lines with a scalar — a double-quoted string (with `""` and the two escapes a path or a sentence needs, `\"` and `\\`), a decimal integer, exactly `true`, `false` or `null`, nothing (null), or a **plain** scalar read as a string only where it is a word of letters, digits, `_`, `-` and `.` that starts with a letter and is not, in any case, one of YAML 1.1's typed words (`yes`, `no`, `on`, `off`, `y`, `n`, `true`, `false`, `null`, `~`); every other plain scalar — a date such as `2026-11-04`, a number with a point or a sign, an octal, hex or sexagesimal form, a word with a space or a colon, one of the typed words in another case — is refused with its line and the advice to double-quote it, as is a single-quoted scalar, which the subset does not read, so that a value `pyyaml`'s resolver would type is never read as a string here while another reader reads it as a date or a boolean; or a flow list `[a, b]` of such scalars; block lists (`- ` items, scalars or mappings); nested mappings and block lists by two-space indentation, at most eight levels deep in all (the schemas need three), a deeper block, mapping or list, refused with its line, so that a hook meets the malformed fallback and never a recursion error; `#` comments, a comment starting at a `#` outside a quoted string that opens the line or follows whitespace, so that `"PR #135"` is text; a byte-order mark tolerated. It refuses a tab in indentation, an anchor, an alias, a tag, a flow mapping (`{a: b}`, alone or inside a list: the subset writes mappings in block form only), a multi-line or block scalar, a duplicate key and a block above 262,144 bytes, each with its line. The migration quotes every string that the plain form would refuse (every date, every value such as `on`, every path with a space) and the empty string, written `""`, since a blank value reads as null, and the templates show them quoted. It is **not** `pyyaml`: every program is a one-shot script on the owner's interpreter and `bridge_env` is the one module they share (D-002's rule); a third-party import there would be the first in the installed programs and would turn a missing package into a failed hook at every session start. `pyyaml` (6.0.3, in `requirements.lock` for seven tests already) is used **in the tests only**: a test parses every fixture header (TASK-046) and every header of this repository's migrated records (TASK-054, under R-063) with `yaml.safe_load` and asserts the subset reader returns the same value, so a header any YAML reader accepts is read the same here, and every plain form the subset refuses unquoted is one `safe_load` types as other than a string (a date, `on`, a number) or one the schemas never write unquoted (a word with a space, a colon or a slash, which the migration quotes), the test pinning that disjunction and not the first half alone. Alternatives considered: `pyyaml` at run time (rejected above); JSON front matter (rejected: a JSON block is unreadable prose and the plan's words are YAML, which Markdown viewers render as front matter); TOML (rejected: `tomllib` reads it on 3.11 and later only and viewers do not render it).
+
+**Writing.** The supervisor writes a header as it writes the record: a new project's records carry them from their templates (the status template in the core's "Project state", the register's format in `design.md`, the plan's increment format in `planning.md`, the brief's in `spec-packager.md`, the index's and the deviations file's in `Diagram-Planning-Conventions.md` §3, the inventory's in "Project state", the run-parameters fence in `run-parameters.md`), and an edit to a prose field edits its header key in the same change, the checker's agreement rule catching an edit that forgot. **Migration** is a program, `records-migrate.py` (new, `bridge/cursor-bridge/`, with its command-reference entry): given a project root (the current folder by default; a given path that is a folder is the root, one at most, a second refused with exit 2) it enumerates the eight kinds' records by its kind rule (the status file, every `docs/BUILD_PLAN*.md`, every `handoff/*.md` other than a report or a resolution file — a report by `bridge_env.HANDOFF_REPORT_NAME`, which admits `<ID>-report.md` and `<ID>-r<N>-report.md` alike, a resolution file by `HANDOFF_RESOLUTION`, so that a report is never read as a brief, ISS-023's class — the inventory, the register, the index, the deviations file, the run parameters), or given record paths it takes those, and writes the header for each that has none from `bridge_env`'s fallback reading of the prose (so the header agrees with the prose by construction), in the subset's own form (the keys in the schema's order; a list in flow form where every item is a short scalar and in block form otherwise, every list of mappings in block form, since the reader refuses a flow mapping; every string the plain form would refuse quoted), each write atomic — the whole file written to a temporary file beside the record and moved into place with `os.replace`, the record read back and checked (`read_header`, `check_header`, the agreement) before the move, so a crash or a full disk leaves the record as it was and never half-written — marks `historic: true` on a plan increment the fallback reads with no Scope or Kind and whose ID a squash subject merged, read from the one merged set `plan-check.py` reads — the one function `bridge_env.squash_merged_ids(folder, PLAN_ID_PATTERN, ref=<base>)`, which `plan-check.py` calls in place of its own `merged_ids` (retired) and the migration calls with its `--base`, both options defaulting to `origin/main` (the function's own default, `main`, stays for its other callers) — read lazily, only where a plan file holds such an increment, so the two programs agree on one checkout; where that set cannot be read (no such ref, a git failure) the plan file is listed `cannot read <path>: merged set unreadable (<reason>)` and nothing is written for it; leaves a record with a header unchanged, refuses a malformed one (exit 2, the run stopping at that record, the records before it written and listed), exits 3 where a read-back fails (the run stopping the same way, that record untouched), and **lists and leaves header-less** a record the fallback refuses (a brief whose Scope holds an ellipsis or is empty, as `parse_scope` refuses it today; a plan whose `Scope:` line holds an unbalanced parenthesis; a given path under none of the eight kinds, `cannot read <path>: not a record kind`, which affects no exit: `cannot read <path>: <reason>`, nothing written, the run going on to the next record, every program reading that record as it does today; a merged brief among them is never repaired, since its prose is the record of what its builder was given, which the migration adds a header above and never rewrites: it stays header-less, read as today, its line standing in every `--check`) and a status file whose header and body together would exceed either of the older caps, 12,000 characters or 120 lines (`cannot write <path>: <n> characters with the header, over the older cap; trim the body first`, or `<n> lines`), each listed by `--check` too; with `--check` it writes nothing and lists what it would write, what it cannot, and what has a header (exit 1 where any enumerated record would be written or cannot be written, a `cannot write` being a record awaiting its trim, and 0 otherwise, a `cannot read` line affecting that exit not at all, and the migration run's own exit the same rule, 1 where a `cannot write` stands and 0 otherwise, a `cannot read` affecting it not at all either, so that a project with a refused merged brief reaches "nothing to write" and exit 0 on both, the listed lines telling the supervisor what to repair; so that one command, `python ~/.claude/cursor-bridge/records-migrate.py --check` from the Workspace root, is the calibration's, TASK-054's and the demonstration's); a `--base` beginning with `-` is refused (exit 2), as the launcher refuses an option-shaped argument. Calibration runs it over a project's records at the next checkpoint, `--check` first, and commits the result as the calibration's checkpoint, append-only, in a records-only commit of its own so the next increment's review diff stays readable, as `project.json` was added at release A2 (`calibrate-bridge.md`, step 4: a record-keeping addition, no gate reopened); a running project's records are migrated so. **This repository's records are migrated the same way**, by the installed program at this project's calibration to the release, after the stamp and the owner's install (the plan addendum's last increment): a session always runs the installed bridge, never the one being edited (INVENTORY, Decisions, "Development data"), and an increment that ran the Workspace copy over this project's live records would run unreleased code on a running project, the thing that decision forbids. **The status file's cap** (`inventory-check.py --max-chars 12000` and `--max-lines 120`) counts the body, the text after the header, since the header is bounded by its schema (its one list, `open_issues`, mirrors a line the cap alr