+### Data model and development data
+
+The bridge keeps no product data; its records are the files above and the JSON files each program owns. The regime is shipped, so the change keeps every record in the field readable and migrates none by force: a record without a header is read as today, for as long as it has none; the migration adds a header and removes nothing; a header's schema version is `1`, and a later schema change reads both versions or migrates again. **If rolled back:** an older release's programs read the prose and never the header, which they see as a block of text at the top — inert but for the effects the release **observes rather than asserts** (ISS-028): the stamp increment (TASK-053) runs the installed release's checkers, the programs a rollback puts back (`~/.claude/cursor-bridge/`, 2026.11.03a at the time of writing), over a scratch copy of this repository's records migrated by the Workspace program (a copy, so the development-data decision holds) and quotes their lines in the CHANGELOG's **If rolled back**; TASK-054 runs them again over the live records once migrated and quotes the lines in `docs/HARDENING.md`, a difference being a text correction that rides with the next release (the type's rule) unless it changes what a project does; the two effects foreseen are `completion-check.py`'s `Satisfies` search over the whole plan matching the header's `satisfies:` lines too (the same IDs, so the same answer; `plan-check.py`'s case-insensitive `FIELD_RX` and `DEMO_RX` match header lines as well, inert only because the header precedes the first stage heading), and `inventory-check.py` counting the status file whole, in characters and in lines, which the migration's refusal of an over-cap file keeps under both caps. `Development data:` as recorded (INVENTORY, Decisions): the Workspace sources under `bridge/` are the dev build; a session runs the installed bridge; the migration's dry run (`--check`) and its tests run on copies under `tests/` and on the fixtures, never on another project'